      <article class="ins-article">
        <div class="ins-source-note">
          <span class="k">In response to</span>
          <span class="body"><a href="https://www.ledinside.com/news/2026/9/2026_09_30_04" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ECOC 2026 Showcases New Optical Communications Launches</a><span class="pub"> &middot; LEDinside (reposting optics.org)</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="ins-tldr">
          <div class="lbl">Key takeaways</div>
          <p>A trade roundup from ECOC 2026 describes an external laser source built as a pluggable module for co-packaged optics. Interoperability, connector handling and how assembly and test divide between the laser and the optical engine are the open questions.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="lede">A <a class="inline" href="https://www.ledinside.com/news/2026/9/2026_09_30_04" target="_blank" rel="noopener">trade roundup from ECOC 2026</a> describes an external laser source built as a pluggable module for co-packaged optics (CPO). An external laser source supplies laser light from outside the switch package and delivers it by fiber to the optical engine. For data center programs, it shows how assembly and test may divide between the laser and the engine.</p>
        <figure class="ins-figure"><div class="img"><img src="~/images/photos/cap-osat-optical-packaging-hero.webp" alt="Optical packaging at a Hana facility"/></div></figure>
        <h2 id="why-would-an-external-laser">Why would an external laser source move into a pluggable module?</h2>
        <p>A pluggable module keeps the laser diodes in a cooler part of the system and allows field replacement. OIF published its External Laser Small Form-Factor Pluggable (ELSFP) agreement in 2023. It places the lasers at the front panel, which OIF describes as the coolest part of the system. A rear multi-fiber blind-mate connector reduces eye-safety risk at high optical power.</p>
        <p>The roundup covers a vendor module in this form factor. It carries eight wavelengths near 1311 nm and 1331 nm, combined by wavelength-division multiplexing, at up to 24 dBm (about 250 mW) per wavelength at the fiber. The vendor states about 12 W of module power and an interface designed to meet applicable Optical Compute Interconnect multi-source agreement requirements. These vendor-stated figures come from a demonstration, without independent measurement.</p>
        <h2 id="where-the-practical-constraints-sit">Where the practical constraints sit</h2>
        <p>A common form factor does not make modules from different suppliers interchangeable. The vendor describes the interface as designed to meet requirements, which is narrower than full compliance, so interoperability with a given optical engine still requires test evidence covering both the laser module and the engine. The vendor also states a ±0.2 nm wavelength accuracy target, so stability over temperature needs qualification.</p>
        <p>Moving the laser outside the package adds a fiber path. The optical loss budget and the cleanliness of each connector end face affect the power reaching the engine. At high power per wavelength, end-face contamination typically calls for inspection control. The same roundup covers other routes, such as microLED links and VCSEL-based die-to-die links, so this is one of several approaches.</p>
        <h2 id="watching">What we're watching</h2>
        <p>These questions will show how the approach develops and where assembly and test work for the laser and engine ends up:</p>
        <div class="ins-watchlist"><ul><li>Do modules from more than one supplier work with the same optical engine, and how is that demonstrated in test results?</li><li>Does the laser stay in a front-panel module in later generations, or move closer to the package as channel counts and power per wavelength change?</li><li>How are blind-mate connector end faces inspected and kept clean in production at these power levels?</li><li>How does the assembly and test flow of the optical engine change when the laser is qualified separately from the engine?</li><li>How do pluggable, on-board and co-packaged implementations share one specification, and when does production volume start?</li></ul></div>
        <h2 id="buyers">Why it matters for manufacturing buyers</h2>
        <p>An external laser source separates laser qualification from optical engine assembly, so a data center program may carry two sets of test limits. Hana's <a class="inline" href="/markets/data-centers">Data Centers market</a> scope includes the control electronics and modules that Hana assembles around thermoelectric cooling devices in optical transceivers. Where a program dual-sources from multiple countries within the same company, each site qualifies separately against the customer's requirements.</p>
